<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dodona</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dodona in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epirus">Epirus</a>, north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, was the oldest oracle of the Greek world, sacred to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeus">Zeus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dione">Dione</a>. Priests interpreted the rustling of a sacred oak, and enquirers wrote their questions on lead tablets, many of which survive. Under the Molossian kings it gained a great <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> and became the religious centre of Epirus.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dodona.jpg" alt="Dodona" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Marcus Cyron · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dodona-Greece-April-2008-107.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>With the rise of the city-states in the 8th century BCE, sanctuaries grew: the first temples were built to house images of the gods, and great sanctuaries shared by all Greeks developed at Olympia, Delphi, Delos and <b>Dodona</b>.</blockquote><a href="/religion/ancient-greek-religion">Ancient Greek religion · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>The ancients themselves placed the poet early: Herodotus, having consulted the Oracle at <b>Dodona</b>, put Homer and Hesiod about 400 years before his own time, which would be around 850 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/work/iliad">Iliad · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>The economy rested on pastoralism, with <b>Dodona's</b> oracle of Zeus as the great religious centre.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-epirus">Kingdom of Epirus</a></li></ul></section>
